WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Friday,

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.280
the thirteenth of November.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.320
This episode is written and read with AI.

4
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.053
President Trump says the US no
longer needs a deal with Iran,

5
00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:15.040
and Brent briefly jumped back to
ninety-four dollars a barrel on the

6
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.560
news. In the US, stocks
built across crude,

7
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.080
gasoline and diesel, and with the
diesel export route to Europe shut

8
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.440
for December, those barrels
are staying at home.

9
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.440
On the markets. Earlier in Asian
trade, Brent had been at ninety-one

10
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.600
dollars thirty, with West Texas
Intermediate at eighty-five dollars thirty,

11
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.760
according to OilPrice dot com.

12
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.960
Crude is still about twenty percent
below its peak of one hundred and

13
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.720
eighteen dollars, set in late October.

14
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.440
US commercial crude stocks rose nearly
four point seven million barrels

15
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.000
in the week to the sixth of
November, according to the EIA.

16
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.960
EU gas storage stood at seventy-one
point two percent full on the eleventh

17
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.320
of November, according to
Gas Infrastructure Europe.

18
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.800
Starting with Iran. On Thursday,
Trump posted that the US has no need

19
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.867
for a deal with Tehran, a
change in tone from Monday,

20
00:01:03.893 --> 00:01:06.520
when he suggested the
war was nearing its end.

21
00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.920
He also claimed Washington controls
Iran's money and the Strait of Hormuz.

22
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:14.480
That is his assertion, and Tehran has
not responded in the reports we've

23
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.787
seen today.

24
00:01:15.813 --> 00:01:19.280
Brent had spent the morning drifting
lower on peace hopes for both Iran

25
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.280
and Ukraine, according
to OilPrice dot com.

26
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.240
The Ukraine side gave the
market little to work with.

27
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.120
Talks in Abu Dhabi on the tenth and
eleventh of November agreed in principle

28
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.333
on a prisoner exchange, but made
no progress towards a ceasefire.

29
00:01:33.547 --> 00:01:36.200
Meanwhile, Gulf shipping
is still taking hits.

30
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.920
A projectile struck the engine room
of the Liberia-flagged crude tanker

31
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.640
Dominica Prosperity off the
United Arab Emirates on Wednesday,

32
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.720
and all twenty-two crew were
evacuated, gCaptain reported.

33
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.000
Oman is also managing a tanker that
ran aground off Al Qibliyah Island,

34
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.120
including what happens to its cargo.

35
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.720
For shipowners, the price swings
are showing up in fuel bills.

36
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.400
Bunker prices have fallen by up to
twenty percent in under two weeks,

37
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.787
according to Splash twenty-four seven.

38
00:02:03.893 --> 00:02:07.520
Turning to US products. Distillate
stocks reversed in the week to the

39
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.200
sixth of November, rising four point
one million barrels to about one

40
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.120
hundred and three million,
according to the EIA.

41
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.960
The week before, they had fallen
by about three point six million.

42
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.400
Gasoline rose one point
eight million barrels.

43
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.560
Total commercial petroleum stocks
were up eleven point four million,

44
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.640
with refineries running at ninety-five
point two percent of capacity.

45
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.080
The catch for Gulf Coast refiners
is that Europe isn't pulling those

46
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.240
barrels. Weaker cash differentials
in Northwest Europe have shut the

47
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.640
transatlantic arbitrage for every
December arrival on Medium Range tankers,

48
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.800
according to Kpler. Late-December
margins on that route fell about five

49
00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.520
dollars thirty a tonne. That leaves
them at minus three dollars thirty.

50
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.120
Kpler reckons a four percent drop
in forward freight would reopen the

51
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.680
route. Until then, the Gulf Coast's
swing diesel stays in the US,

52
00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.840
and it's adding to domestic stocks.

53
00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.400
Separately, the EIA raised its Brent
forecasts for this year and next

54
00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.080
in its latest Short-Term Energy
Outlook, though the report we've seen

55
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.440
doesn't give the new figures.

56
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.480
Over in Asia, Gulf crude has overtaken
Russian barrels in India for the

57
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.160
first time since the war began.

58
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.720
So far in November, India has taken
about two point one million barrels

59
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.880
a day from the Gulf, according to Kpler.

60
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.520
Russia supplied about
one point eight million.

61
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.800
The shift reflects recovering
flows through Hormuz,

62
00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.120
and Russian crude losing
its price advantage.

63
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.920
Gulf grades are getting cheaper
as they fight for buyers.

64
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.640
The Dubai spread between the first
and third months has collapsed from

65
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.280
about eleven dollars a
barrel to about one dollar,

66
00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.880
according to Kpler, and Gulf producers
have cut their official selling

67
00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.680
prices. Prompt Agbami cargoes into
Ningbo are about four dollars a barrel

68
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.760
more competitive than Murban.

69
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.800
Other Asian refiners are
shopping further afield.

70
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.200
Traders and shipbrokers,
who weren't named,

71
00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.520
say Sinopec and SK Energy have bought
African and Brazilian grades as

72
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.240
long-haul freight eases, Reuters reported.

73
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.080
Russian sellers may now face pressure
to deepen discounts if they want

74
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.640
Indian demand back.

75
00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.160
Also from Reuters, the Association
of Southeast Asian Nations has agreed

76
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.160
a joint crude stockpile. The storage
will sit in Indonesia and Thailand.

77
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.840
Now to European gas. Storage slipped
about a third of a percentage point

78
00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.480
on the day, according to
Gas Infrastructure Europe.

79
00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.760
Germany was at fifty-eight
percent in the previous update.

80
00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.320
Asian spot liquefied natural gas
is trading above thirty dollars per

81
00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.840
million British thermal units,
according to OilPrice dot com.

82
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.240
Even so, Rystad's winter outlook
doesn't call a further rally.

83
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.960
It lays out as many paths down as up.

84
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.840
Europe is still leaning on Russian
liquefied natural gas ahead of the

85
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.840
ban in January. The EU paid an estimated
eight point seven billion euros

86
00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.720
for Yamal cargoes from January to October,

87
00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.960
according to Urgewald and Kpler.

88
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.560
That is already more than
it paid in all of last year.

89
00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.600
Finally, supply news
from the Atlantic basin.

90
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.440
ExxonMobil has taken a final investment
decision worth twelve and a half

91
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.280
billion dollars on Longtail,
according to World Oil.

92
00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.760
It is the eighth development
on the Stabroek block,

93
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.840
and Exxon's first gas-condensate
project offshore Guyana.

94
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.920
Longtail is designed to process one
point two billion cubic feet of gas

95
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.160
a day. It is due to produce up to two
hundred and fifty thousand barrels

96
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.360
a day of condensate,
starting in twenty thirty.

97
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.520
In West Africa, Nigeria pumped one
point five million barrels a day of

98
00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.120
crude in October, according
to its upstream regulator.

99
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.880
That keeps it at its OPEC quota
for a sixth straight month.

100
00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.280
What to watch next. Daily EU storage
readings from Gas Infrastructure

101
00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.080
Europe run through the
nineteenth of November.

102
00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:54.080
The US Jones Act emergency
waiver expires on the fifteenth.

103
00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.600
And on the eighteenth, the EIA
weekly for the week to the thirteenth

104
00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:00.360
of November brings the
next distillate figure.

105
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.160
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
